nd stops at its fuel, oxygen or temperature limit. It does not directly burn living colonies, spread wildfire or unlock fire-adapted life. Heat can affect life afterward. Wet ground cannot ignite. Use Find dead organic matter to navigate to existing fuel (dry eligible patches are preferred), then inspect Life snapshot. Wet fuel remains wet; use Water − if you deliberately want to expose it. Pause to hold conditions, then reopen or refresh this panel: water can return while time runs. Nothing is automatically drained or seeded, and ignition does not promise a benefit.</p></details></dialog><dialog id="life-sheet" aria-labelledby="life-title"><div class="sheet-heading"><h2 id="life-title">Life in this patch</h2><button id="close-life">Close</button></div><p id="life-summary" aria-live="polite"></p><p id="life-habitat"></p><button id="watch-patch">Watch this patch · 10 seconds</button><p class="life-note">Observe actual activity; does not unpause or change speed.</p><div id="fine-water" hidden><p>Selected patch water · fine adjustment. Time keeps running unless paused.</p><button id="water-minus">−0.1 m water</button> <button id="water-plus">+0.1 m water</button><details id="advanced-water"><summary>Set depth or shape basin</summary><div id="target-water" hidden><p>Set this patch to an explicit depth. This supplies or removes water once, not automatic care.</p><label for="water-target">Target depth (metres, 0–5)</label><input id="water-target" type="number" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="5" step="any" value="0.5"><button id="water-target-apply">Set depth</button> <button id="water-shallow">Set 0.5 m</button><button id="make-pond">Shape basin + add water</button><p>Shapes up to 19 terrain patches: a level floor and dry rim raised at least 17 m above it. Supplies 15 m water to the floor and drains the rim once. Existing life is not replaced; changed terrain/water can affect it. No automatic refill.</p></div></details><p id="water-feedback" aria-live="polite"></p></div><button id="refresh-life">Refresh snapshot</button><section aria-label="Current colony shares"><h3>Who lives here</h3><p id="life-colony-shares"></p><p class="life-note">Current biomass shares, not winners or growth rates.</p></section><details id="competition-details"><summary>Competition and living traits</summary><section id="competition-panel" hidden aria-labelledby="competition-title"><h3 id="competition-title">Competition at a glance</h3><p id="life-competition" style="white-space:pre-line"></p></section><section id="living-traits" hidden aria-label="Living traits"><b>Living traits · current colonies</b><p id="living-traits-copy"></p><p class="life-note">Potential comes from inherited structure plus living support. Compatibility is not measured photosynthesis or a growth guarantee; dry land is not supported. Biomass trend is reported separately.</p></section></details><details id="cohort-details"><summary>Colony ancestry and detailed traits</summary><div id="life-cohorts"></div></details><details id="condition-details"><summary>Resources and habitat details</summary><p id="life-conditions"></p></details><details id="feeding-details"><summary>Find recorded feeding</summary><button id="find-feeding">Find recorded feeding</button><button id="cancel-feeding" hidden>Cancel search</button><p id="feeding-result" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><p class="life-note">Amber dots mark living grazing-capable microbes with past feeding, not eating now.</p></details><details id="life-help"><summary>How to read this ecosystem</summary><p id="life-glossary" class="life-note"></p><p class="life-note">Colony IDs are serial identifiers, not a count of living colonies or species. Births since start includes colonies that later died. Generation counts descent steps from a seeded ancestor. Parent IDs may refer to colonies no longer alive. Net change includes births, losses, movement and interventions.</p><p class="life-note">Watch reports actual photosynthesis, grazing and biological carbon returned to air. The return excludes movement respiration and god tools; this is not a complete carbon budget.</p></details></dialog><section id="guide" hidden><button id="close-guide">Close</button><h2 id="guide-heading">Sandbox habitat guide</h2><div id="goal-card" aria-live="polite"><h3 id="goal-title">Start a habitat</h3><p id="goal-text">Start the game, then check your habitat for a suggested next step.</p></div><div class="guide-actions"><button id="check-habitat">Check habitat</button><button id="guide-land">Find land</button><button id="repeat-guide">First steps</button></div><p><span id="mode-explanation">This sandbox starts with plants and rabbits. It does not yet evolve simple life into new plant or animal groups. Suggestions reflect the current planet, not required levels or guaranteed survival.</span></p><details id="manual"><summary>Controls and field notes</summary><div id="origin-fields" hidden><p>Turquoise mats mark real microbial biomass. Tap <b>Habitat</b> to find shallow water; <b>Seed</b> introduces an existing colony. <b>Nutr. +</b> is a deliberate external nutrient input, not food created by the ecosystem.</p><p>Simple seeds need 0.2–50 m water above 0°C, usable light and resources. Evolved producers with actual paid support and inherited structure can exploit thinner liquid films; the 15–20 cm transition also permits partial aquatic growth. No photosynthesis occurs when dry or frozen. Grazing and movement still require aquatic conditions. Inspect shows carbon biomass, nutrients and the dominant living colony. Descendants can develop aquatic producer forms: cushion, rosette, ribbon, branching and canopy. Shapes follow inherited traits and actual support tissue; these are not terrestrial plant species or animals.</p><p><b>Habitat care:</b> Use <b>Find habitat → Shape nursery → Seed colony → Watch life</b> for a shallow starting patch. Nursery shapes a 5 m center beside a 15 m reservoir and dry rim; it supplies water once, not new organisms. Press Play if paused. The older level 15 m basin is still available in Life snapshot. Inspect shallow patches as water changes. For routine replenishment, a <b>Water +</b> tap adds 1 m to the selected patch. <b>Water −</b> removes up to 1 m per tap from only the selected patch, or 5 m per second while painting; it never drains neighbors. Both water brushes are external interventions, not rainfall or evaporation. For precision, open <b>Life snapshot</b> and choose <b>Set 0.5 m</b>. Both supply water once: there is no automatic refill or guaranteed evolution.</p></div><div id="sandbox-fields"><p><b>Read the map:</b> tan is bare land, blue is water, green is vegetation, and orange dots mark living rabbits. Close-up sprouts show real vegetated patches, not individual plant counts; they grow with coverage and shrink when grazed. Rabbit models stand on the surface when you zoom close.</p><p><b>1. Find your land.</b> Tap <b>Land</b> below the game to jump to dry ground. Use <b>＋</b> to get close and <b>Globe</b> to return to a whole-planet view.</p><p><b>2. Shape a habitat.</b> Use Land +/− and Water +/−. Dry land supports rabbits; deep water does not. Pause while sculpting. Water − removes up to 1 m per tap from the selected patch only (5 m per second held), never neighboring water. Water and land brushes respond to how long you hold, not simulation speed. Inspect shows water depth.</p><p><b>3. Plant the land.</b> Choose Plants and paint patches of vegetation. Rabbits eat it, so establish food before adding animals.</p><p><b>4. Add rabbits.</b> Choose Rabbits, then tap suitable land. Watch the population and inspect the surface temperature. This prototype currently has vegetation and rabbits, not a full multi-species ecosystem.</p></div><p><b>Always daylight</b> keeps the side you are viewing lit while the climate simulation continues. You can change the view in the planet menu.</p><p>Tap menu buttons and the tool buttons inside the game. Choose a tool, then touch the planet to use it.</p><p><b>Pinch with two fingers</b> to zoom smoothly. In <b>Navigate</b> mode, drag one finger to explore and tap to use a menu or selected tool. Switch to <b>Paint</b> to brush plants or terrain by dragging. Pinching never finishes as a tool tap. Zoom +/− also works.</p><p>Landscape gives you more room. Full screen hides the browser bars. The game automatically resizes when you rotate.</p><p><b>More → Save / Load:</b> Save keeps one planet in this browser on this device, including its ecosystem. It is not a cloud backup. Wait for the Saved message before closing. Clearing site data or using a private browsing session can remove it. Load replaces your unsaved world.</p><p><b>Mobile detail:</b> New browser planets use a lighter grid for smoother play and smaller saves. Existing saved worlds keep their original grid detail when loaded.</p><p>Pause, settings and tool details are now in the game, above the planet. Open <b>More → Speed / fast-forward</b> to choose a target of 1, 6 or 24 simulated hours per real second. The top pace button also cycles these targets. Achieved speed is measured separately and may be lower on your device. New worlds start with the 6-hour target; saved worlds keep their requested pace. Pause to sculpt or inspect. Faster time also dries water faster, so watch your habitat.</p></details><a href="qa.html">Executed gameplay tests</a> · <a href="source.html">GPL source</a> · <a href="LICENSE">License</a></section><aside id="gameplay-measure-status" hidden aria-live="polite"><span id="gameplay-measure-message"></span><button id="cancel-gameplay-measure">Cancel</button></aside></main><script src="copy-report.js"></script><script src="renderer-info.js"></script><script src="gameplay-measure.js"></script><script src="touch-controls.js"></script><script src="stella-storage.js"></script><script src="life-trend.js"></script><script src="patch-watch.js"></script><script>
